WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.687
Good morning, and thank you.

2
00:00:01.687 --> 00:00:05.104
Digital identity has two
faces: the public who sign in

3
00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:08.675
to an agency's services, and
the workforce who serve them.

4
00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:12.021
Both rest on the same data
discipline: one person,

5
00:00:12.021 --> 00:00:17.375
one key the agency controls, and every
fact owned by one authoritative source.

6
00:00:17.375 --> 00:00:20.922
I'll show that discipline
through the workforce face,

7
00:00:20.922 --> 00:00:25.437
because Federal HR 2.0 puts it on
participating agencies' calendars.

8
00:00:25.437 --> 00:00:29.046
I have one proposal, and I'll
take it through four questions,

9
00:00:29.046 --> 00:00:32.954
the way I'd take it to leadership:
why it matters, what we should do,

10
00:00:32.954 --> 00:00:37.313
who has to be involved, and how we'd
carry it out and know it's working.

11
00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:38.780
Why.

12
00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:42.627
Based on current visibility,
many agencies do not operate

13
00:00:42.627 --> 00:00:46.555
a comprehensive enterprise
HR-driven identity lifecycle.

14
00:00:46.555 --> 00:00:51.034
In the processes I have observed, many
workforce identity changes remain

15
00:00:51.034 --> 00:00:55.367
dependent on tickets or manual
coordination, and that costs three ways.

16
00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:59.184
Access outlives the job: when
someone leaves or changes roles,

17
00:00:59.184 --> 00:01:03.001
removal can wait for a ticket,
and a stale account is a security

18
00:01:03.001 --> 00:01:06.593
violation that can enable a
breach of the public's records.

19
00:01:06.593 --> 00:01:10.733
Federal standards require revoking
a separated person's credentials,

20
00:01:10.733 --> 00:01:13.892
and a ticket-dependent process
struggles to meet that.

21
00:01:13.892 --> 00:01:17.320
Records we can't trust: in
the processes I have observed,

22
00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:21.814
the phone book is wrong until someone
files a ticket, and every change of

23
00:01:21.814 --> 00:01:26.842
manager means the organization, the manager
and tool access are updated by hand.

24
00:01:26.842 --> 00:01:29.131
Approvals route through that same data.

25
00:01:29.131 --> 00:01:34.792
And cost and delay: many joiners, movers
and leavers become help-desk work.

26
00:01:34.792 --> 00:01:37.354
But the bigger why is where
the mission is heading.

27
00:01:37.354 --> 00:01:40.300
Take an illustrative
shared-service scenario:

28
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:44.830
a cross-agency contact center, one
front door for two agencies' customers.

29
00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:47.442
It's an example, not an approved program.

30
00:01:47.442 --> 00:01:50.429
The hard part of an idea
like that isn't the phones:

31
00:01:50.429 --> 00:01:53.267
cloud contact centers
make the front end easy.

32
00:01:53.267 --> 00:01:54.456
It's the back end.

33
00:01:54.456 --> 00:01:55.655
Who is this agent?

34
00:01:55.655 --> 00:01:57.767
Which agency's records may they see?

35
00:01:57.767 --> 00:02:01.518
And does their access end the
day they leave, in both agencies?

36
00:02:01.518 --> 00:02:04.992
A standardized, HR-driven
identity supplies the trusted

37
00:02:04.992 --> 00:02:08.318
facts needed to answer those
questions consistently.

38
00:02:08.318 --> 00:02:13.441
And if we do nothing, HR moves to
Core HCM, identity moves to Entra ID,

39
00:02:13.441 --> 00:02:18.580
and we rebuild the same manual
process twice, on new platforms.

40
00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:20.380
What should we do?

41
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:25.662
I've weighed four realistic options against
what leadership actually has to balance:

42
00:02:25.662 --> 00:02:29.680
the mission and the public we serve,
security, cost and resilience.

43
00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.880
Compliance matters, but on its
own it isn't a reason to invest.

44
00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:37.026
A, keep ticket-driven
identity: no new investment,

45
00:02:37.026 --> 00:02:40.330
but the risk and the manual
work stay where they are.

46
00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.130
That's the cost of doing nothing.

47
00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:47.530
B, automate inside the directories with
scripts and exports: quick and cheap.

48
00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:49.473
It can automate selected changes,

49
00:02:49.473 --> 00:02:53.567
but without a governed authority
model and complete lifecycle events,

50
00:02:53.567 --> 00:02:58.355
it risks propagating inconsistent data
and being rebuilt when the source changes.

51
00:02:58.355 --> 00:03:04.855
C, wait for HR 2.0 and integrate afterward:
defensible, and less work this year.

52
00:03:04.855 --> 00:03:08.734
But program material describes
interconnections occurring during

53
00:03:08.734 --> 00:03:12.480
the onboarding period, so waiting
means retrofitting after go-live.

54
00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:17.791
D, start now: HR as the source of truth,
in OPM's standard personnel data,

55
00:03:17.791 --> 00:03:22.818
designed into both the HR onboarding
and the Entra ID migration.

56
00:03:22.818 --> 00:03:26.583
It costs design effort up front
and needs shared governance,

57
00:03:26.583 --> 00:03:28.842
which is why it's only partial on cost.

58
00:03:28.842 --> 00:03:34.318
The judgment call: if HR 2.0 slipped
a long way, C becomes reasonable.

59
00:03:34.318 --> 00:03:38.633
But D's first step — data standards,
ownership and the person key —

60
00:03:38.633 --> 00:03:44.050
is no-regret either way, and it can start
now from the HR data the agency already

61
00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:46.166
has, subject to data profiling,

62
00:03:46.166 --> 00:03:50.105
with the source switched to
Core HCM when it arrives.

63
00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:55.210
I propose option D, phased,
starting with the no-regret steps.

64
00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:56.235
Four reasons.

65
00:03:56.235 --> 00:04:00.711
Mission and the public: access to the
public's records should end when a person's

66
00:04:00.711 --> 00:04:02.846
role ends, automatically, the same day;

67
00:04:02.846 --> 00:04:06.839
the people who serve the public should
have the right access on day one;

68
00:04:06.839 --> 00:04:11.178
and shared services like the illustrative
contact center become possible,

69
00:04:11.178 --> 00:04:15.447
because participating agencies would
describe their people the same way.

70
00:04:15.447 --> 00:04:20.030
Cost: we build it once, while both
platforms are being rebuilt anyway,

71
00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:24.285
and retire ticket work for routine
joiners, movers and leavers.

72
00:04:24.285 --> 00:04:27.484
Security: modern access
decisions, Zero Trust,

73
00:04:27.484 --> 00:04:33.377
increasingly depend on authoritative
identity, device and contextual attributes,

74
00:04:33.377 --> 00:04:36.408
and the identity attributes, organization,

75
00:04:36.408 --> 00:04:39.523
position, status and
supervisor, come from HR.

76
00:04:39.523 --> 00:04:43.611
So the accuracy of HR data
drives the accuracy of access.

77
00:04:43.611 --> 00:04:48.347
The federal mandates point the same way,
but they're the floor, not the reason.

78
00:04:48.347 --> 00:04:51.409
And it gives the inspector
general's perennial question —

79
00:04:51.409 --> 00:04:54.472
how do you know every account
belongs to a current person?

80
00:04:54.472 --> 00:04:55.684
— a standing answer.

81
00:04:55.684 --> 00:05:01.110
Resilience: by building on OPM's standard
data rather than one vendor's fields, and

82
00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:06.535
on a person key the agency controls, the
design survives the next platform change.

83
00:05:06.535 --> 00:05:10.629
I propose an existing enterprise
ID, such as a Mainframe PIN,

84
00:05:10.629 --> 00:05:14.723
as the candidate, subject to
formal identifier validation.

85
00:05:14.723 --> 00:05:16.047
Two honest limits.

86
00:05:16.047 --> 00:05:18.608
HR 2.0 covers federal employees only,

87
00:05:18.608 --> 00:05:24.495
so a contractor authority decision is
required: I propose evaluating suitability

88
00:05:24.495 --> 00:05:27.225
records, PIV sponsorship and Core HCM's

89
00:05:27.225 --> 00:05:30.723
contingent-worker records
as candidate sources.

90
00:05:30.723 --> 00:05:33.985
And we should build only what
HR is authoritative for.

91
00:05:33.985 --> 00:05:39.182
The upside: participating civilian agencies
are moving toward a common Core HCM

92
00:05:39.182 --> 00:05:43.449
platform and federal personnel
standards, so a reusable identity

93
00:05:43.449 --> 00:05:48.248
model could reduce reinvention
across agencies and shared services.

94
00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:50.375
Who has to be involved.

95
00:05:50.375 --> 00:05:53.989
This is an organizational change
as much as a technical one,

96
00:05:53.989 --> 00:05:55.612
so it matters who owns what.

97
00:05:55.612 --> 00:05:59.612
HR owns the personnel data and
its quality, at the source.

98
00:05:59.612 --> 00:06:04.301
When the data is wrong, it gets fixed
in HR, not patched in the directory.

99
00:06:04.301 --> 00:06:10.700
And the OPM HR 2.0 program owns the platform,
the standards and the interconnection.

100
00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:17.050
The CIO's identity team uses that data to
run Entra ID, Active Directory and access.

101
00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:21.325
Security sets the Zero Trust
policy and owns the leaver path.

102
00:06:21.325 --> 00:06:25.487
And the help desk moves from routine
tickets to handling the exceptions.

103
00:06:25.487 --> 00:06:27.162
Then the guards and partners.

104
00:06:27.162 --> 00:06:31.320
The privacy office is in from the
start, because using HR data for

105
00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:35.713
identity is a disclosure that needs
a routine use and minimization.

106
00:06:35.713 --> 00:06:40.774
For people HR doesn't cover, contractor
sponsors, credentialing, acquisition,

107
00:06:40.774 --> 00:06:44.649
suitability and HR systems each
may hold part of the record;

108
00:06:44.649 --> 00:06:49.474
the authority decision identifies
which source owns each required fact.

109
00:06:49.474 --> 00:06:53.587
And partner agencies need the same
standard if we're going to share services.

110
00:06:53.587 --> 00:06:58.210
My role, as an enterprise data
architect, is to own the data standards,

111
00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:02.749
the logical model and the person
key: every attribute gets one name,

112
00:07:02.749 --> 00:07:05.638
one definition and one accountable owner.

113
00:07:05.638 --> 00:07:08.373
I'd chair the data working
group for the effort,

114
00:07:08.373 --> 00:07:13.266
review the HR 2.0 integrator's design
artifacts against those standards, and carry

115
00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:18.376
the standard to the interagency committees
and working groups where it gets adopted.

116
00:07:18.376 --> 00:07:22.715
The person model and its attributes
also become a governed piece of the

117
00:07:22.715 --> 00:07:27.575
agency's data and metadata inventory,
which auditors increasingly ask for.

118
00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:30.934
This is the data architecture underneath,

119
00:07:30.934 --> 00:07:34.655
and one principle carries it:
the person is the durable entity.

120
00:07:34.655 --> 00:07:38.792
Accounts, affiliations, assignments,
identifiers and credentials

121
00:07:38.792 --> 00:07:42.930
have different lifecycles but
resolve to the same governed person.

122
00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:48.005
I propose keying that person on an
enterprise person key the agency controls.

123
00:07:48.005 --> 00:07:51.368
In the proposed canonical
model, conversion creates a

124
00:07:51.368 --> 00:07:54.880
new affiliation for the same
person, so history survives.

125
00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:58.014
The right-hand side shows
where each piece would live;

126
00:07:58.014 --> 00:08:00.080
the backup slides carry the detail.

127
00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:05.127
The standard behind it: one name, one
definition and one owner per attribute,

128
00:08:05.127 --> 00:08:09.905
and the DBAs build from the logical
model, not from a vendor's fields.

129
00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:12.772
Here is how I propose we carry it out.

130
00:08:12.772 --> 00:08:13.447
Data.

131
00:08:13.447 --> 00:08:18.687
One governed crosswalk maps OPM's standard
data elements into that person model,

132
00:08:18.687 --> 00:08:23.223
so when HR changes platforms only
the crosswalk's source side changes.

133
00:08:23.223 --> 00:08:28.709
Validated personnel-action mappings then
initiate the identity events — joiner,

134
00:08:28.709 --> 00:08:34.359
mover, leaver — using a proposed classification
that the data owners validate first.

135
00:08:34.359 --> 00:08:39.435
Contractors come into the same model once
the contractor authority decision is made.

136
00:08:39.435 --> 00:08:40.410
Security.

137
00:08:40.410 --> 00:08:44.729
The leaver path blocks access first,
within the approved threshold,

138
00:08:44.729 --> 00:08:47.660
and then reconciles
every downstream action.

139
00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:50.188
The integration runs with least privilege,

140
00:08:50.188 --> 00:08:54.822
using managed identities and secretless
authentication wherever supported.

141
00:08:54.822 --> 00:08:58.376
And anything that fails goes
to a quarantine with an owner;

142
00:08:58.376 --> 00:09:00.523
nothing resolves itself silently.

143
00:09:00.523 --> 00:09:01.659
Operations.

144
00:09:01.659 --> 00:09:05.290
We start now, from the HR
data the agency already has,

145
00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:10.648
subject to data profiling, and switch
the source to Core HCM when it arrives.

146
00:09:10.648 --> 00:09:13.845
We run in shadow mode first,
comparing what HR says

147
00:09:13.845 --> 00:09:17.122
with what the directories
hold, and fixing the gaps.

148
00:09:17.122 --> 00:09:19.498
We cut over one population at a time.

149
00:09:19.498 --> 00:09:22.440
And we close the ticket
path for a population

150
00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:25.461
only when the data proves
HR is the sole source.

151
00:09:25.461 --> 00:09:28.345
The products on the slide
are one realization;

152
00:09:28.345 --> 00:09:31.985
the agency-owned model
remains stable if they change.

153
00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:35.272
How we'll know it's
working, and what I'd need.

154
00:09:35.272 --> 00:09:38.229
Six measures, each baselined
in the first phase,

155
00:09:38.229 --> 00:09:41.488
and each one a business
measure leadership can read:

156
00:09:41.488 --> 00:09:44.520
time from separation to
blocked access, in hours,

157
00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:49.143
against an agency-approved threshold;
credential and access revocation,

158
00:09:49.143 --> 00:09:51.417
reconciled to verified completion,

159
00:09:51.417 --> 00:09:56.798
where one credential-specific constraint
is the federal requirement to terminate a

160
00:09:56.798 --> 00:10:01.800
PIV card that can't be collected within
eighteen hours; accounts tied to a person,

161
00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:06.682
with orphaned accounts trending to zero;
joiner and mover tickets going down,

162
00:10:06.682 --> 00:10:11.118
with access ready on day one; the
manager and organization in the

163
00:10:11.118 --> 00:10:15.722
directory matching HR; and every
exception with an owner and an age.

164
00:10:15.722 --> 00:10:21.022
As a byproduct, the same data is evidence
for the controls auditors ask about.

165
00:10:21.022 --> 00:10:23.946
I'm asking leadership to
make three decisions:

166
00:10:23.946 --> 00:10:26.408
name identity as an HR 2.0 data consumer,

167
00:10:26.408 --> 00:10:29.870
authorize the cross-functional
data-governance work,

168
00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:35.486
and require assumptions to be validated by
accountable owners before implementation.

169
00:10:35.486 --> 00:10:39.768
That lets the agency modernize the
platforms and the lifecycle together,

170
00:10:39.768 --> 00:10:42.647
rather than carrying the
ticket process forward.

171
00:10:42.647 --> 00:10:43.410
Thank you.

172
00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:45.510
I look forward to your questions.

173
00:10:46.310 --> 00:10:52.026
HR-driven identity is one piece of a larger
set of modernizations I've designed over

174
00:10:52.026 --> 00:10:54.926
the last several years:
Entra ID migration,

175
00:10:54.926 --> 00:10:58.157
next-generation DNS and
IP address management,

176
00:10:58.157 --> 00:11:01.471
field office WAN and
data-center consolidation,

177
00:11:01.471 --> 00:11:07.768
the TIC 3.0 Cloud and Branch Office cases,
the client-server to hybrid cloud path,

178
00:11:07.768 --> 00:11:12.573
SQL Server identity, the move from
Group Policy to Intune and Azure Arc,

179
00:11:12.573 --> 00:11:18.372
and continuous compliance against
SCuBA, BOD 25-01 and FedRAMP 20x.

180
00:11:18.372 --> 00:11:23.427
They share the same data discipline you
just saw: a named authority for every fact,

181
00:11:23.427 --> 00:11:26.747
a governed model, and evidence
produced by operation.

182
00:11:26.747 --> 00:11:28.559
I'm happy to go into any of them.

183
00:11:28.559 --> 00:11:33.657
Two companion decks go deeper on this
one: the Contractor Identity Source deck,

184
00:11:33.657 --> 00:11:36.323
on who is authoritative for a contractor,

185
00:11:36.323 --> 00:11:41.498
and the original HR-Driven Identity deck,
with the full model, lifecycle and plan.
